# Go Development Best Practices for TIL Project

## Code Style & Formatting

- Always run `gofmt` and `goimports` before committing code
- Follow standard Go formatting conventions (1TBS style braces, etc.)
- Use `golangci-lint` or similar tools to catch style issues
- Prefer explicit error handling over panics
- Use meaningful variable and function names that are clear in context

## Error Handling

- Always handle errors explicitly - never ignore them with `_`
- Use `fmt.Errorf` with `%w` verb for error wrapping to preserve error chains
- Return errors as the last return value from functions
- Use sentinel errors (errors.New) for expected error conditions
- Document error conditions in function comments
- Prefer returning errors over calling fatal/panic functions in library code

Example:
```go
if err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("failed to read config: %w", err)
}
```

## Testing

- Write tests for all exported functions and critical logic
- Use table-driven tests for multiple test cases
- Use `t.Helper()` in test helper functions
- Place tests in `*_test.go` files in the same package
- Use descriptive test names: `TestFunctionName_Scenario_ExpectedResult`
- Test both success and error cases
- Use `testify` assertions when appropriate, but prefer standard library when simple

Example:
```go
func TestNewPage(t *testing.T) {
    t.Helper()
    tests := []struct {
        name  string
        title string
        want  string
    }{
        {"valid title", "Test Title", "test-title"},
    }
    for _, tt := range tests {
        t.Run(tt.name, func(t *testing.T) {
            // test implementation
        })
    }
}
```

## Documentation

- Document all exported functions, types, and packages
- Start comments with the name of the thing being documented
- Use complete sentences in documentation
- Include examples for complex functions using `// Example` comments
- Document package purpose in package comment

Example:
```go
// NewPage creates and returns a new Page instance with the given title.
// The page will be saved to the specified target directory.
func NewPage(title string, targetDir string) *Page {
```

## Package Organization

- Keep packages focused and cohesive
- Prefer small, focused packages over large monolithic ones
- Use `internal/` for packages that should not be imported by other projects
- Group related functionality together
- Avoid circular dependencies

## Code Organization

- Order imports: standard library, third-party, local packages (separated by blank line)
- Use constructors (New* functions) for struct initialization when validation is needed
- Prefer `any` over `interface{}` for empty interfaces
- Use constants for magic numbers and repeated strings
- Group related constants together
- Use meaningful constant names, not abbreviations
- **Function ordering**: Exported functions must always come before private functions in a file
- **Exported function ordering**: Exported functions must be written in alphabetical order (functions starting with 'A' before 'B', etc.)
- **Private function ordering**: Private functions must be written in alphabetical order (functions starting with 'a' before 'b', etc.)

## Performance

- Prefer `strings.Builder` for string concatenation in loops
- Use `make()` with capacity when slice size is known
- Avoid premature optimization - profile first
- Use `sync.Pool` for frequently allocated objects if profiling shows benefit
- Prefer value receivers unless method needs to modify receiver or receiver is large

## Concurrency

- Use `context.Context` for cancellation and timeouts
- Prefer channels for communication between goroutines
- Use `sync` package primitives (Mutex, WaitGroup, etc.) when appropriate
- Document goroutine lifecycle and ownership
- Avoid goroutine leaks - ensure all goroutines can exit

## Dependencies

- Minimize external dependencies
- Prefer standard library solutions when available
- Keep dependencies up to date and secure
- Use `go mod tidy` to clean up unused dependencies
- Document why external dependencies are needed

## CLI-Specific Guidelines

- Use `flag` package for command-line flags (or `cobra`/`urfave/cli` for complex CLIs)
- Provide clear, helpful error messages to users
- Use exit codes appropriately (0 for success, non-zero for errors)
- Support `-h`/`--help` flags
- Consider adding `-version` flag for version information

## Security

- Validate and sanitize user input
- Use `filepath` package for file path operations (not `strings`)
- Be careful with file permissions (avoid world-writable files)
- Don't log sensitive information
- Use secure defaults

## Project-Specific Patterns

- Use constructors (New* functions) for creating instances
- Error constants should be defined near where they're used
- Status messages should be constants when reused
- Prefer explicit configuration over magic values
- Use the existing logging patterns (`src.Info`, `src.Progress`, etc.) for consistency

## Git & Version Control

- **Commit messages**: All commit messages must start with the robot-head emoji (🤖) before any other emoji or commit message text
- Write clear, descriptive commit messages
- Use present tense ("Add feature" not "Added feature")

## Code Review Checklist

- [ ] Code follows Go formatting standards
- [ ] All errors are handled explicitly
- [ ] Tests are included for new functionality
- [ ] Documentation is updated for exported symbols
- [ ] No unnecessary dependencies added
- [ ] Code is readable and maintainable
- [ ] Performance implications considered (if applicable)
- [ ] Functions are ordered correctly (exported before private, alphabetical within each group)
